<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwame Nkrumah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kwame Nkrumah was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>'s independence movement and the first prime minister and president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, which became independent in 1957. He chose the name Ghana in tribute to the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, though the old empire lay far to the north-west. The choice reflects the empire's lasting symbolic importance in African history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah.jpg" alt="Kwame Nkrumah" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: USSR Post · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kwame_Nkrumah_on_a_Soviet_stamp_(80th_anniversary_of_his_birth)_1989_CPA_6101.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kwame Nkrumah was born Francis Nwia Kofi Nkrumah on 21 September 1909 at Nkroful, a small village in the Nzema area of the southwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, close to the frontier with the French colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>. He was his mother's only child. His father, a goldsmith, worked at Half Assini, and Nkrumah went to the elementary school run there by a Catholic mission. After ten years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in May 1945 and enrolled at the London School of Economics, then at University College London, where his supervisor A. J. Ayer thought he lacked an analytical mind, and he did not complete his law studies at Gray's Inn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> constitution of 1946 gave Africans a majority on the legislative council for the first time, and the United Gold Coast Convention (UGCC), the colony's first true political party, was founded in August 1947. It hired Nkrumah to run it, and he sailed home from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> in November 1947. In April 1949 his supporters pressed him to leave the UGCC, and on 12 June 1949 he announced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-peoples-party">Convention People's Party</a> (CPP), choosing the word convention, he said, to carry the masses with us. He became prime minister in 1952 and worked closely with the British governor, Arden-Clarke, who told the civil service to support the new government.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> became independent on 6 March 1957, the first British African colony to gain majority-rule independence, and the celebrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> drew over 100 reporters and photographers. His government was socialist and nationalist and funded industrial and energy projects. Accra hosted a series of pan-African meetings: the First Conference of Independent States in April 1958 and the All-African Peoples' Conference in December 1958, with representatives of 62 nationalist organisations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Unrest followed independence, and in December 1957 an act banned regional or tribal political parties; opposition parties then formed the United Party under Kofi Abrefa Busia. After an alleged assassination plot and worsening economic conditions Nkrumah's government became more authoritarian, and in 1964 a constitutional amendment made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> with Nkrumah as president for life. While he was on a state visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in February 1966, the National Liberation Council, led by Joseph Arthur Ankrah, overthrew him in a coup. He lived the rest of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conakry">Conakry</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> on 27 April 1972. He is remembered as a leading advocate of pan-Africanism and a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kwame Nkrumah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Gold Coast gained independence in 1957, <b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> chose this empire's name to assert a pre-colonial African tradition of statehood and sophistication, seven centuries after the original kingdom had ceased to exist.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> won his seat in the Legislative Assembly election of February 1951 while in jail for sedition; the governor, Sir Charles Arden-Clarke, released him and invited him to form a government as 'leader of government business'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Burma and Ceylon followed in 1948, and in the Gold Coast <b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> drew on Gandhi’s example for his campaign of “positive action” in 1950.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…founders were Josip Broz Tito, the Marxist–Leninist president of Yugoslavia, whose initiative led to the Belgrade conference; Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt; Jawaharlal Nehru, prime minister of India; <b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> of Ghana; and Sukarno of Indonesia, host of the Bandung Conference, who gave the movement a significant boost.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> argued in Africa Must Unite (1963) that only a continental union government, with a common market, currency and defence, could make independence real and resist what he called neo-colonialism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>…Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson trace present differences in wealth and government to colonial institutions, and the word neocolonialism, used by Jean-Paul Sartre in 1956 and by <b>Kwame Nkrumah</b>, Ghana’s first president, in Neo-Colonialism: The Last Stage of Imperialism (1965), names economic and political control that outlasted formal empire.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nkroful, in what is now <a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=4.96,-2.32&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Nkroful on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4486 1978 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kwame Nkrumah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4936 2248h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4936 2248h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=4.96,-2.32&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
